!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Escaping
description: backslash carries the literal reading; quotes carry only spaces
- |
  A key may itself contain a metacharacter — `: [ ] * & # ~ \`, the query characters
  `? ! ( ) < > = |`, or an all-dots segment `..` / `...`. Escape it with a
  **backslash**, which suppresses the pointer meaning of the next character; a key
  containing a **space** is quoted instead. Backslash escaping carries the
  literal-versus-interpreted distinction that quotes cannot: in JSON5 and YAML `'`
  and `"` are interchangeable delimiters — `*".."` and `*'..'` are the same string,
  both meaning parent.
- !!yo
  mime:  *..: text/html: x          # "/" is no metachar — rides bare
  colon: *schedule: 09\:30          # a literal ":" inside the key
  space: *: tags: 'дорожный знак'   # a key with a space — quoted
  dots:  *\.\.                      # the literal key ".."
  star:  *\*boss                    # the literal key "*boss"
- |
  What the slash gained by leaving the metacharacter set: MIME-type keys, date keys
  (`01/02/2026`), and URL-ish keys ride bare; a legacy `\/` still reads as the
  literal slash — `\X` makes any X literal. Pointers and queries share this one
  lexical space: a key escaped for one is escaped identically for the other.
